thanks to powerful telescopes  astronomers know what our universe looks  like today and they have a pretty good  idea of how it got started but what  happened in between how galaxies like  our Milky Way were created and how they  evolved isn't as clear  that's where simulations can help  scientists have now made one of the most  detailed simulations of the evolution of  the universe it spans almost 350 million  light years but includes enough detail  to zoom in on individual galaxies as  they grow the simulation starts 12  million years after the Big Bang this  view shows a chunk of the simulation  that is 30 million light years across  gravity is pulling dark matter into  dense pouches connected by wispy trails  that resemble a spider's web that's what  astronomers call the cosmic web in this  view the denser the dark matter of the  Pinker the color.this where stars and  galaxies are starting to form then  ordinary matter enters the picture we're  now looking at gases swirling in and  among galaxies which are growing bigger  and starting to collide cooler gases are  blue where stars form green gas is  warmer while red and white gases are  extremely hot and surround the most  violently active galaxies supermassive  black holes sit at the center of these  galaxies as the black holes pool in  matter around them they heat the  material and blow out gas in explosive  bursts of white  as time passes the explosions spread  warm gas throughout space  as the simulation progresses it also  shows how the elements such as hydrogen  helium carbon and oxygen spread  throughout space the diffusion of these  elements shown in purple is essential to  the development of stars planets and  even life approaching the universe as it  is today the simulation switches back to  dark matter it shows how the tightly  packed nuggets of dark matter in the  early universe have expanded over  billions of years to become the massive  galaxy clusters and superclusters such  as the Virgo supercluster we see today
 

